    {
      "beat_id": "B03",
      "act": "ABSTENTION",
      "narration_text": "When all three uncertainty measures spike — wide intervals, high dropout variance, models disagreeing — the system does something unusual. It abstains. Rather than force a low-confidence forecast, it flags the prediction as unreliable and steps aside. About twelve percent of predictions trigger abstention. The payoff: accuracy on the remaining eighty-eight percent improves by eight points. Knowing when to say I don't know turns out to be its own kind of intelligence.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "build",
